INT. NIGHT-FLIGHT CARGO DEPOT, GRAND CENTRAL POST OFFICE - NIGHT

A cavernous sorting hall, the last shift of a century-old mail depot. Conveyor belts rattle in a long copper-colored light. Tin parcels, rope-bound crates, wire baskets of letters stacked like wheat.

CORINNE ABADI-VEGA (30s, sleeves rolled, ink on her cuffs) stands at a sorting desk with a clipboard, lit by a green banker's lamp. She hasn't looked at the clipboard in minutes.

Across the hall, a forklift hums to a stop. A man in a gray coat steps down from it and walks toward her, slowly, as if the floor might not hold.

THEO MARCHETTI-OYELOWO (30s, rumpled, a cardboard tube under one arm) stops six feet from the desk. He opens his mouth, shuts it. The belt rattles between them.

CORINNE
You were supposed to be in Lisbon.

THEO
The flight got cancelled.

CORINNE
Planes get cancelled in Lisbon every week. You came here.

THEO
I came here.

She puts the clipboard down and aligns it with the edge of the desk, three times, until it is exactly parallel. He watches her do it.

THEO (CONT'D)
You still do that.

CORINNE
You still turn up.

THEO
Corinne.

CORINNE
(over him)
I got your letter.

THEO
(over her)
Did you read it?

Both stop. A beat. The belts clatter.

CORINNE
Go on.

THEO
You first.

CORINNE
Go on, Theo.

THEO
Did you read it?

CORINNE
Twice. I kept the envelope.

He pulls the cardboard tube from under his arm and holds it out to her without a word. She doesn't take it. A package slides off a belt and thumps onto a pile. Neither of them looks.

THEO (CONT'D)
It's the house. The one on the river. I drew it.

CORINNE
You can't draw.

THEO
I can't. I tried anyway.

She takes the tube. Pops the cap. Draws out a roll of tracing paper, spreads it across the desk under the green lamp, and holds the corners down with a stapler and a roll of tape. A lopsided house, a lopsided tree, two figures at a lopsided table.

CORINNE
(softly)
That's not our table.

THEO
It's the one I want.

CORINNE
Our table has three legs and a book under the fourth.

THEO
I'd keep the book.

A giggle escapes her. She covers her mouth, appalled. He steps forward. She steps back, against the desk, lamp swaying. The shadows of both of them swing across the hall.

CORINNE
(quiet)
You left.

THEO
I know.

CORINNE
You didn't leave. You evaporated.

THEO
I know.

CORINNE
And now you've drawn me a house.

THEO
And I'd like to live in it with you. If the offer stands.

A pause. The conveyor stops with a hydraulic sigh and the hall goes silent. The two stand very close.

CORINNE
(whispering)
I hate this about you.

THEO
(whispering)
I know.

CORINNE
(together)
Stay.

THEO ^
(together)
Stay.

They hear each other. Both freeze, then both laugh, helplessly, and her forehead drops onto his shoulder. His hand finds her back. Behind them the belts shudder and start to turn again.
